/**
 * \page ophys Acquiring Optical Physiology Data 🔬
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section ophys_overview Overview
 *
 * AqNWB supports the acquisition of raw imaging data from one-photon and two-photon
 * microscopes. The data is described by the following neurodata_types:
 *
 * | Type | Description |
 * |------|-------------|
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImagingPlane "ImagingPlane" | Metadata about an imaging plane (e.g., location, indicator, excitation wavelength), stored in `/general/optophysiology`. Owns one or more \ref AQNWB::NWB::OpticalChannel "OpticalChannel" objects and links to the \ref AQNWB::NWB::Device "Device" used for imaging. |
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::OpticalChannel "OpticalChannel" | An optical channel (e.g., emission wavelength) used to record from an imaging plane. |
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::TwoPhotonSeries "TwoPhotonSeries" | Frames recorded with a two-photon microscope. Links to its imaging plane. |
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::OnePhotonSeries "OnePhotonSeries" | Frames recorded with a one-photon microscope. Links to its imaging plane. |
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImageSeries "ImageSeries" | The general image series type that the series above inherit from. Also supports images stored in external files. |
 *
 * All types are initialized from a nested `Spec` struct, e.g.,
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::TwoPhotonSeries::Spec "TwoPhotonSeries::Spec", which is validated before any
 * data is written. The convenience functions in the \ref AQNWB::NWB::Ophys "Ophys" namespace create the
 * objects at the locations defined by NWB.
 *
 * \note
 * The optical physiology types are part of an optional module that is enabled by default. It can be
 * disabled at build time via the CMake option `-DAQNWB_ENABLE_OPHYS=OFF`. The preprocessor
 * definition `AQNWB_HAS_OPHYS` indicates that the module is available.
 *
 * \section ophys_workflow Recording two-photon imaging data
 *
 * \subsection ophys_workflow_nwbfile 1. Create the NWBFile
 *
 * As usual, we first create the I/O and the \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile "NWBFile" (see \ref workflow).
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testOphysWorkflowExamples.cpp example_ophys_nwbfile_snippet
 *
 * \subsection ophys_workflow_device 2. Create the Device
 *
 * Next, we create the \ref AQNWB::NWB::Device "Device" that represents the microscope.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testOphysWorkflowExamples.cpp example_ophys_device_snippet
 *
 * \subsection ophys_workflow_imaging_plane 3. Create the ImagingPlane
 *
 * The \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImagingPlane "ImagingPlane" describes where and how the data is acquired.
 * The optical channels are added to the spec via
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImagingPlane::Spec::withOpticalChannel "withOpticalChannel" and are created
 * together with the imaging plane.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testOphysWorkflowExamples.cpp example_ophys_imaging_plane_snippet
 *
 * \subsection ophys_workflow_series 4. Create the TwoPhotonSeries
 *
 * The data of an image series is a 3D `[frame, x, y]` (or 4D `[frame, x, y, z]`) array.
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::Ophys::getDefaultImageSeriesDataConfig "getDefaultImageSeriesDataConfig"
 * creates a dataset configuration that is extendable in time and stores one frame per chunk.
 * Here, the frames are acquired at a regular rate, so we specify the starting time and rate instead
 * of recording timestamps for each frame.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testOphysWorkflowExamples.cpp example_ophys_two_photon_series_snippet
 *
 * \subsection ophys_workflow_record 5. Record the frames
 *
 * During the recording, we append frames via
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::Ophys::writeImageSeriesFrames "writeImageSeriesFrames", using the recording
 * object index of the series, or directly via
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImageSeries::writeFrames "ImageSeries::writeFrames". The frames are contiguous
 * arrays in row-major order with the data type of the dataset.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/testOphysWorkflowExamples.cpp example_ophys_record_snippet
 *
 * \section ophys_rois Recording regions of interest (ROIs) and their responses
 *
 * Acquisition systems that segment the images online can also store the regions of interest (ROIs) and
 * their responses (e.g., fluorescence or dF/F). By convention, these are stored in the "ophys"
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::ProcessingModule "ProcessingModule":
 *
 * | Type | Description |
 * |------|-------------|
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::PlaneSegmentation "PlaneSegmentation" | A table with one row per ROI of an imaging plane. The ROIs are defined by the `image_mask` column (a weight per pixel) or by the `pixel_mask` / `voxel_mask` columns (a list of pixels or voxels with weights per ROI). Stored in the \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImageSegmentation "ImageSegmentation" container. |
 * | \ref AQNWB::NWB::RoiResponseSeries "RoiResponseSeries" | The responses of the ROIs over time. The `rois` \ref AQNWB::NWB::DynamicTableRegion "DynamicTableRegion" references the rows of the PlaneSegmentation. Stored in the \ref AQNWB::NWB::Fluorescence "Fluorescence" or \ref AQNWB::NWB::DfOverF "DfOverF" container. |
 *
 * The functions \ref AQNWB::NWB::Ophys::createPlaneSegmentation "createPlaneSegmentation" and
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::Ophys::createRoiResponseSeries "createRoiResponseSeries" create the processing module
 * and containers if they do not exist.
 *
 * \snippet tests/testOphysRois.cpp example_ophys_rois_snippet
 *
 * Instead of an `image_mask`, the ROIs can also be defined by sparse pixel (or voxel) masks, i.e., a
 * list of (x, y, weight) entries per ROI. These columns use compound data types (see
 * \ref AQNWB::IO::BaseDataType::Compound "BaseDataType::Compound").
 *
 * \snippet tests/testOphysRois.cpp example_ophys_pixel_masks_snippet
 *
 * \section ophys_motion_correction Storing motion-corrected images
 *
 * A \ref AQNWB::NWB::CorrectedImageStack "CorrectedImageStack" stores the motion-corrected frames, the
 * x/y translation of each frame, and a link to the original series. Its spec contains the nested specs
 * of the `corrected` \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImageSeries "ImageSeries" and the `xy_translation`
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::TimeSeries "TimeSeries", which are created by `initialize`.
 *
 * \snippet tests/testOphysDeferred.cpp example_motion_correction_snippet
 *
 * \section ophys_stimulus_images Stimulus images and other image types
 *
 * - \ref AQNWB::NWB::Images "Images" is a collection of \ref AQNWB::NWB::GrayscaleImage "GrayscaleImage",
 *   \ref AQNWB::NWB::RGBImage "RGBImage", and \ref AQNWB::NWB::RGBAImage "RGBAImage" objects with an optional
 *   order (`order_of_images`).
 * - \ref AQNWB::NWB::IndexSeries "IndexSeries" stores indices into the images of an Images collection,
 *   e.g., the order in which stimulus images were presented.
 * - \ref AQNWB::NWB::OpticalSeries "OpticalSeries" stores image data that is presented or recorded,
 *   e.g., a movie, and \ref AQNWB::NWB::ImageMaskSeries "ImageMaskSeries" stores a mask applied to another
 *   ImageSeries.
 *
 * \snippet tests/testOphysDeferred.cpp example_images_snippet
 *
 * \section ophys_ogen Recording optogenetic stimulation
 *
 * Optogenetic stimulation is described by an
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::OptogeneticStimulusSite "OptogeneticStimulusSite" (stored in `/general/optogenetics`),
 * which links to the stimulation \ref AQNWB::NWB::Device "Device", and an
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::OptogeneticSeries "OptogeneticSeries" with the applied power in watts. The
 * convenience functions are in the \ref AQNWB::NWB::Ogen "Ogen" namespace.
 *
 * \snippet tests/testOgen.cpp example_ogen_snippet
 *
 * \note
 * The optogenetics types are part of an optional module that is enabled by default. It can be
 * disabled via the CMake option `-DAQNWB_ENABLE_OGEN=OFF`. The preprocessor definition
 * `AQNWB_HAS_OGEN` indicates that the module is available.
 *
 * \section ophys_reading Reading the data
 *
 * The data can be read via the read methods of the types, e.g.,
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::TwoPhotonSeries::readImagingPlane "TwoPhotonSeries::readImagingPlane" (see \ref read_page).
 *
 * \note
 * When linking AqNWB statically, the linker only includes the classes that the application uses
 * directly. Applications that read files with types they do not use directly should call
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::registerAllTypes "registerAllTypes()" before reading such that
 * \ref AQNWB::NWB::RegisteredType::create "RegisteredType::create" can create objects of all types.
 */
